Pashupatas Lord Krishna faced the Brahmastra with another Brahmastra, the Vayuastra with the mountainastra,
the Agniastra with the rainastra, and Shivji's own Pashupatastra with his own weapon
Narayanastra. Mohayitva tu girisham jrumbhantrashtraren jrumbitam. Of arrows, of heads, of swords, and of spears. 14. Meaning of the word

Mohayitva—By enchanting; Tu—Then; Girisham—To Shivji; Jhumbhan-astrenna—With the weapon that brings fear; Jhumbhitam—By bringing fear; Baanasya—Of the arrow; Pratnam—To the army; Shaurih—Krishna; Jaghaan—Started to kill; Asi—Sword; Gada—Mace;
Ishubhim—And with arrows.
.

After enchanting Shiva by the Jambhastra weapon, Krishna began to kill Banasura's army with his sword, mace, and arrows. Skandha, Pradyumna, and Ardhmana, together.
